//@version=6
// ============================================================================
// PULLBACK IN AN UPTREND — the board's `pull` row, on a chart.
//
// MIRRORS kitelab/entries.py signal(..., entry == "pull") + simulate(), which
// is row `pull` of the 36-row board and section "Pullback in an uptrend" of
// output/reports/strategy_report.pdf.
//
// THE RULE
//   ENTRY  buy at the close of the first daily bar where BOTH are true:
//            close > SMA(close, 200)   the long trend is up, and
//            close < SMA(close, 20)    the last few weeks are down.
//          Only on the RISING EDGE — false yesterday, true today. The
//          condition can stay true for weeks; without the edge the rule would
//          re-enter every session and pay a spread a day to hold one position.
//   STOP   close - 3 x ATR(14), measured on the entry bar. ATR ONLY.
//          The board trades this entry against two stop widths -- the wide
//          ATR one here, and a narrow one at the entry bar's own low -- and
//          until 2026-09-23 this script offered both behind a settings
//          dropdown. The narrow arm was taken out on the user's instruction
//          ("for pull use atr only"), so that what is on the chart is one
//          strategy rather than two. The removed arm is not a secret: it is
//          still `pull|own` on the board, still a sheet in the workbook
//          below, and still in this file's git history at commit 8217a9d.
//   EXIT   sell at the close, whichever comes first:
//            - a close AT OR BELOW the stop, or
//            - 60 sessions held.
//          CLASS CONVENTION: the stop is checked at CLOSES only. A wick that
//          dips below it during the bar does NOT sell. (A broker's resting
//          stop order would fire intrabar — a different, also valid strategy,
//          but a manual end-of-bar backtester can only check closes.) The stop
//          is checked BEFORE the 60-session cap, so a bar that trips both is
//          charged the worse of the two.
//   SIZE   risk a % of a FIXED capital per trade: the distance to the stop
//          decides the share count, capped by what the capital can buy. Fixed,
//          not compounding, because that is what the lab's sizing.position()
//          does.
//   SKIP   if the stop is at or above the entry price there is nothing to risk
//          and nothing to size against, so the signal is skipped, not filled.
//
// WHAT IT MEASURED (dashboard built 2026-09-19, 276 scenarios per arm,
// costs on). Median return per year against simply buying and holding the
// same universe:
//     pull|atr3   -1.27 pts/yr   behind hold in 164 of 276 scenarios
//     pull|own    -7.05 pts/yr   behind hold in 220 of 276 scenarios  (removed)
// `pull|atr3` is the BEST of all 36 rows on the board — and it still loses to
// buy-and-hold. Nothing here is a recommendation to trade it.
//
// MAKING THE NUMBERS MATCH THE WORKBOOK
// The target is the `pull - 3xATR stop` sheet of
// output/measurements/hg_vs_pull_RELIANCE.xlsx. On NSE:RELIANCE, daily, with
// the defaults below, the Strategy Tester should read:
//
//     Total closed trades     56
//     Net Profit              Rs 22,364.75      <- the sheet's PROFIT column
//     first entry             2006-10-26  at 140.00, 121 shares
//     last entry              2025-12-09  at 1529.40, 14 shares
//     largest single buy      Rs 26,166.80
//
// Four settings are what make that hold, and changing any one breaks it:
//
//   1. THE DATE WINDOW. The lab's price file for RELIANCE starts 2006-01-02
//      and ends 2026-09-02; TradingView carries the stock back to the 1990s
//      and forward to today. Left alone the chart would take a decade of
//      trades the workbook never saw. The window inputs below fence the
//      entries into the lab's range. Bars OUTSIDE it are still loaded and
//      still feed the averages — which is right, the SMA 200 on 2006-10-26
//      is built from bars before it.
//   2. COMMISSION 0 (hard-coded in the declaration; if you want to play with
//      it, the box is in Settings -> Properties, not in this script's own
//      Inputs tab). The Strategy Tester then reports the sheet's GROSS
//      "Profit" column. The sheet's separate "Net Profit" column subtracts
//      real Zerodha delivery charges (brokerage, STT, stamp, exchange, SEBI,
//      GST, DP) which came to Rs 2,593.80 here — 0.166% of turnover, and
//      NOT reproducible by Pine's flat percentage. Match the gross, read the
//      net off the sheet.
//   3. CAPITAL 1,00,000 AT 1% RISK. The class account, which is the book the
//      workbook was written on. The board itself runs this rule at 2,00,000
//      and 10,00,000, so the Strategy Tester's rupees are not the board's.
//   4. A DAILY CHART, adjusted for splits and bonuses (TradingView's
//      default). RELIANCE has had three 1:1 bonuses since 2006; Kite and
//      TradingView both adjust for them but not always to the same paisa, so
//      expect the oldest trades to agree in shape and drift a little in
//      price. Recent years should match exactly.
//
// TWO PLACES IT STILL CANNOT MATCH EXACTLY, both small:
//   1. Re-entry timing. The lab resumes scanning the bar AFTER an exit; here
//      pyramiding = 0 blocks an entry while a position is open. Same effect
//      except on the single bar an exit and a fresh signal coincide.
//   2. A trade still open when the data runs out is DROPPED by the lab and
//      shown as an open position by the Strategy Tester. Compare closed
//      trades; TradingView lists any open one separately.
//
// (ATR seeding used to be on this list. It is not a real difference here:
// the lab seeds its Wilder average on the first true range and Pine seeds on
// the first 14, but the gap decays by 13/14 a bar and the first RELIANCE
// trade is 200 bars in, by which point the two agree past six decimals.)
//
// WHERE TO SEE THE RESULTS: the Strategy Tester panel at the bottom.
// TradingView computes net profit, the equity curve and max drawdown itself,
// which is what makes this an independent check on the Python. For the lab's
// GROSS numbers, set commission to 0 in Settings -> Properties.
//
// USE IT ON A DAILY CHART. The 200 and 20 were measured on daily bars; on any
// other timeframe the script takes no trades and says so.
//
// IF THE STRATEGY TESTER SAYS "this report requires trade data" it means zero
// trades were taken. Read the DIAGNOSTICS PANEL in the top right: it counts
// every fresh signal and sorts it into exactly one bucket, so the row shaded
// red is the reason. The usual causes, in order of how often they happen:
//   - the chart is not daily (row 1),
//   - the symbol has fewer than 200 daily bars loaded, so SMA 200 never
//     forms and the setup can never be true (rows 2 and 3),
//   - "capital < 1 share": the stock costs more than the Capital input, so
//     the size works out to zero. Raise Capital in the settings.
// Turn the panel off once you are getting trades.
// ============================================================================

// --- strategy declaration ---------------------------------------------------
// process_orders_on_close = true is the line that matters most: an order placed
// on a bar FILLS AT THAT BAR'S CLOSE. The default would fill it at the NEXT
// bar's open instead, which is a different strategy (the lab calls that
// NEXT_OPEN_FILLS and leaves it off).
strategy("Pullback in an Uptrend (kitelab)",
     overlay              = true,
     initial_capital      = 100000,
     pyramiding           = 0,                 // never add to an open position
     calc_on_every_tick   = false,             // judge completed bars only
     process_orders_on_close = true,
     commission_type      = strategy.commission.percent,
     // ZERO, so the Strategy Tester reports the workbook's GROSS Profit
     // column. It is a bare literal and not an input on purpose: strategy()
     // types this argument `const float`, which an input is not, so
     // `commission_value = input.float(...)` will not compile. Nothing is
     // lost -- TradingView puts a commission box in Settings -> Properties,
     // which is the right place to experiment with it anyway. See MAKING THE
     // NUMBERS MATCH above for why the lab's charges are not a flat percent.
     commission_value     = 0.0)

// --- inputs -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Exposed so anyone can experiment; every default is the tested board value.
slowInput  = input.int(200, "Slow SMA — the trend filter", minval = 2)
fastInput  = input.int(20,  "Fast SMA — the dip", minval = 2)
atrLenIn   = input.int(14, "ATR length", minval = 1)
atrMultIn  = input.float(3.0, "ATR multiple", minval = 0.1, step = 0.5,
     tooltip = "3 is the board value. ATR — average true range — is the " +
       "average size of a daily bar, so 3 x ATR is a stop three ordinary " +
       "days' movement below the close: wide enough that normal noise does " +
       "not reach it.")
holdInput  = input.int(60, "Max hold, sessions", minval = 1)
riskInput  = input.float(1.0, "Risk % per trade", minval = 0.1, step = 0.5)
useWindow  = input.bool(true, "Fence entries into the lab's date range",
     tooltip = "The lab's RELIANCE file runs 2006-01-02 to 2026-09-02. " +
       "TradingView has more history than that at both ends, and without " +
       "this the trade counts cannot agree. Bars outside the window still " +
       "feed the averages; only ENTRIES are blocked.")
// PLAIN MILLISECOND LITERALS, not timestamp(). input.time types its default
// `const int`, and timestamp() is `simple int` in BOTH its overloads -- the
// numeric one and the string one -- so either spelling fails to compile. A
// literal is the only const form. The Inputs tab still draws these as a date
// picker, so nobody has to read the numbers; they are, in IST:
//     1136140200000  =  2006-01-02 00:00   the first bar of the lab's file
//     1788373740000  =  2026-09-02 23:59   the last bar of the lab's file
fromInput  = input.time(1136140200000, "Entries from")
toInput    = input.time(1788373740000, "Entries until")
capInput   = input.float(100000, "Capital the sizing uses", minval = 1000,
     tooltip = "FIXED, not the running equity — the lab sizes against a " +
       "constant so a good run cannot inflate later position sizes. " +
       "1,00,000 at 1% is the class account, the same book as the workbook " +
       "output/measurements/hg_vs_pull_RELIANCE.xlsx, so the Strategy " +
       "Tester and that spreadsheet can be read side by side.")

// --- the two averages -------------------------------------------------------
// Both SIMPLE, not exponential, and each needs its own full length of history:
// the lab's rolling(N, min_periods=N) is silent for a stock's first 200
// sessions rather than guessing, and ta.sma returns na over the same stretch.
slowMa = ta.sma(close, slowInput)
fastMa = ta.sma(close, fastInput)

// --- timeframe guard --------------------------------------------------------
tfOk = timeframe.isdaily

// --- the date window --------------------------------------------------------
// ENTRIES only. The averages, the ATR and an already-open position all ignore
// this, exactly as the lab does: it reads every bar in the file and simply has
// no bars outside it.
inWindow = not useWindow or (time >= fromInput and time <= toInput)

// --- the signal -------------------------------------------------------------
// `setup` is the rule itself. `cond` is the rule AFTER the timeframe guard.
// They are kept apart on purpose so the diagnostics panel can tell "the setup
// never happened" from "the setup happened and the guard blocked it".
setup = not na(slowMa) and not na(fastMa) and close > slowMa and close < fastMa
cond  = tfOk and setup
fresh = cond and not cond[1]          // the RISING EDGE only

// --- the stop this bar would set --------------------------------------------
atrNow   = ta.atr(atrLenIn)
stopCand = close - atrMultIn * atrNow
perShare = close - stopCand           // what one share puts at risk

// --- sizing -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Whole shares. Two ceilings: the risk budget and what the capital can buy.
byRisk = perShare > 0 ? math.floor(capInput * riskInput / 100 / perShare) : 0
byCap  = close > 0 ? math.floor(capInput / close) : 0
qty    = math.min(byRisk, byCap)

// --- state carried across bars ----------------------------------------------
var float stopLevel = na
var int   entryBar  = na

// Every signal ends in exactly one of these buckets, so the counters below
// always add up to nSignal. A strategy that takes no trades is then never
// silent about why.
var int nSetup    = 0   // bars where the rule was true (guard ignored)
var int nSignal   = 0   // fresh rising edges that passed the guard
var int nEntry    = 0   // ... that became a trade
var int nHeld     = 0   // ... skipped: a position was already open
var int nOutside  = 0   // ... skipped: outside the lab's date window
var int nWarmup   = 0   // ... skipped: inside the ATR warm-up, no stop drawable
var int nNoRisk   = 0   // ... skipped: stop at or above the entry price
var int nTooSmall = 0   // ... skipped: the capital could not afford one share

if setup
    nSetup += 1

// --- entry ------------------------------------------------------------------
if fresh
    nSignal += 1
    if not inWindow
        nOutside += 1
    else if strategy.position_size != 0
        nHeld += 1
    else if na(stopCand)
        nWarmup += 1
    else if perShare <= 0
        nNoRisk += 1
    else if qty < 1
        nTooSmall += 1
    else
        strategy.entry("pull", strategy.long, qty = qty)
        stopLevel := stopCand
        entryBar  := bar_index
        nEntry    += 1

// --- exit -------------------------------------------------------------------
// From the bar AFTER entry, and the stop is tested FIRST, so a bar that trips
// both the stop and the hold limit is charged the worse of the two.
if strategy.position_size > 0 and not na(entryBar) and bar_index > entryBar
    if close <= stopLevel
        strategy.close("pull", comment = "stop (close)")
    else if bar_index - entryBar >= holdInput
        strategy.close("pull", comment = str.tostring(holdInput) + "-session limit")

// --- what you see -----------------------------------------------------------
plot(slowMa, "SMA 200", color = color.new(color.blue, 0),   linewidth = 2)
plot(fastMa, "SMA 20",  color = color.new(color.orange, 0), linewidth = 1)
plot(strategy.position_size > 0 ? stopLevel : na, "Stop",
     color = color.new(color.red, 0), style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 2)

// The dip inside the rise, shaded: above the slow average, below the fast one.
bgcolor(cond ? color.new(color.teal, 90) : na, title = "Setup active")
plotshape(fresh, "Fresh signal", shape.triangleup, location.belowbar,
     color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.tiny)

// --- diagnostics ------------------------------------------------------------
// TradingView's Strategy Tester says only "this report requires trade data"
// when a strategy takes no trades, which is the least useful sentence it could
// say. This panel names the reason instead. Turn it off once trades appear.
showDiag = input.bool(true, "Show the diagnostics panel")

var table diag = table.new(position.top_right, 2, 11,
     border_width = 1, frame_width = 1)

// Built as three parallel lists and drawn in one loop: a Pine function that
// only calls table.cell returns nothing, and a void-returning helper is a
// compile error waiting to happen.
if barstate.islast and showDiag
    barsHere = bar_index + 1
    keys  = array.new_string(0)
    vals  = array.new_string(0)
    warns = array.new_bool(0)

    array.push(keys, "chart timeframe")
    array.push(vals, timeframe.period + (tfOk ? "  OK" : "  NOT DAILY"))
    array.push(warns, not tfOk)

    array.push(keys, "bars on the chart")
    array.push(vals, str.tostring(barsHere))
    array.push(warns, barsHere < slowInput)

    array.push(keys, "SMA " + str.tostring(slowInput) + " ready?")
    array.push(vals, na(slowMa) ? "no" : "yes")
    array.push(warns, na(slowMa))

    array.push(keys, "days the setup was true")
    array.push(vals, str.tostring(nSetup))
    array.push(warns, nSetup == 0)

    array.push(keys, "fresh signals")
    array.push(vals, str.tostring(nSignal))
    array.push(warns, nSignal == 0)

    array.push(keys, "  -> became trades")
    array.push(vals, str.tostring(nEntry))
    array.push(warns, nEntry == 0)

    array.push(keys, "  -> outside the date window")
    array.push(vals, str.tostring(nOutside))
    array.push(warns, false)

    array.push(keys, "  -> already in a trade")
    array.push(vals, str.tostring(nHeld))
    array.push(warns, false)

    array.push(keys, "  -> ATR warm-up")
    array.push(vals, str.tostring(nWarmup))
    array.push(warns, false)

    array.push(keys, "  -> stop above entry")
    array.push(vals, str.tostring(nNoRisk))
    array.push(warns, false)

    array.push(keys, "  -> capital < 1 share")
    array.push(vals, str.tostring(nTooSmall))
    array.push(warns, nTooSmall > 0)

    for i = 0 to array.size(keys) - 1
        bg = array.get(warns, i) ? color.new(color.red, 20) : color.new(color.gray, 25)
        table.cell(diag, 0, i, array.get(keys, i), text_color = color.white,
             text_halign = text.align_left,  text_size = size.small, bgcolor = bg)
        table.cell(diag, 1, i, array.get(vals, i), text_color = color.white,
             text_halign = text.align_right, text_size = size.small, bgcolor = bg)
